        "\n",
        "#### First, the quadrants validate\n",
        "\n",
        "| Quadrant | A | B | C | D | E |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| **Blue Ocean** | 8.4% | 5.0% | 20.7% | 42.5% | 23.3% |\n",
        "| Low Sugar, Low Protein | 3.1% | 6.3% | 24.2% | 48.1% | 18.4% |\n",
        "| High Sugar + High Protein | 0.5% | 0.4% | 5.4% | 39.9% | 53.7% |\n",
        "| **Sugar Trap** | 0.1% | 0.1% | 2.6% | 19.9% | **77.3%** |\n",
        "\n",
        "The blue ocean has the highest A+B share; the sugar trap is 77.3% grade E. An external,\n",
        "regulator-adjacent algorithm that knows nothing about my 10g/10g thresholds independently orders\n",
        "the quadrants exactly as I did. That is genuine corroboration.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Then, the problem\n",
        "\n",
        "| Segment | n | A or B | Median salt |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Blue Ocean — all categories | 10,410 | 13.5% | 1.50g |\n",
        "| **Blue Ocean — Chips & Savoury (my recommendation)** | **5,993** | **9.2%** | **1.78g** |\n",
        "| Blue Ocean — Nuts & Seeds | 1,614 | 20.0% | 1.07g |\n",
        "\n",
        "**My recommendation scores worst of the three.** Blue-ocean savoury snacks earn an A or B only\n",
        "9.2% of the time — below the blue ocean average, and less than half the rate of Nuts & Seeds.\n",
        "Over 65% are D or E.\n",
        "\n",
        "**The cause is salt.** Chips & Savoury has the highest median salt of any category at\n",
        "1.40g/100g — 10× Chocolate (0.14g), 23× Confectionery (0.06g). Blue-ocean savoury products are\n",
        "saltier still, at **1.78g**. Nutri-Score penalises salt heavily, so a product can clear both of\n",
        "my axes and still be graded D, because the third variable — the one the nutrient matrix cannot\n",
        "show — undoes it.\n",
        "\n",
        "*I tested whether protein and salt are coupled within blue-ocean savoury, the intuition being\n",
        "that protein arrives via cheese, cured meat and salted gluten. The correlation is weak,\n",
        "**r = 0.105** — the data does not support that mechanism, though looking only within a\n",
        "high-protein subset restricts the range and likely attenuates it. **The salt problem is real\n",
        "descriptively; its cause is not established here.** The reformulation constraint stands either\n",
        "way.*\n",
        "\n",
        "### What this changes\n",
        "\n",
        "**It does not overturn the recommendation.** Chips & Savoury still has 6,126 blue-ocean products\n",
        "against ~1,650 for nuts, 17,370 low-sugar/low-protein products sitting one variable away, and\n",
        "25,541 products of category scale against 2,954. The gap is real and the category is right.\n",
        "\n",
        "**But it converts a two-variable brief into a three-variable one:**\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Target 15g protein, under 5g sugar, and under 1.0g salt.**\n",
        "\n",
        "That third number is the hard part, and it is now the *actual* R&D problem: existing blue-ocean\n",
        "savoury products sit at 1.78g median salt, so hitting the protein target at under 1.0g means\n",
        "departing from how the category currently builds these products rather than copying it.\n",
        "\n",
        "**This also strengthens the secondary case for Nuts & Seeds** as a launch or halo SKU: 20% A/B\n",
        "and 1.07g salt — the profile achieved without a salt penalty. It lacks the volume to be the\n",
        "platform, but it demonstrates the target is reachable.\n",
        "\n",
        "Without this check, the client could have hit both stated targets and shipped a product graded\n",
        "D — competing on a health platform against confectionery that grades no worse. The positioning\n",
        "would have collapsed at the packaging stage, after R&D had spent the money.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why I added this\n",
        "\n",
        "The brief asked me to find the gap. It did not ask whether the product I was recommending would\n",
        "survive its own label. Nutri-Score was sitting unused in the dataset — externally defined,\n",
        "independent of every threshold I chose, and computed over the exact products in my\n",
        "recommendation. It cost one column.\n",
        "\n",
        "**It found that my two-axis analysis was projecting away the variable that decides whether the\n",
        "product works.** Three hypotheses tested across this section, three came back weaker than\n",
        "expected — reported as found. That is worth more than a third chart confirming what I already\n",
        "believed."
      ],
